<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Royal and Pontifical University of Mexico</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1551–1865</span> · Also known as Royal University of Mexico</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Royal and Pontifical University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> was founded by a decree of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1551 and opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in 1553, one of the first universities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Modelled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>, it taught theology, law, medicine and the arts and trained the clergy and officials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>. Closed and reopened several times after independence, it was finally abolished in 1865.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/royal-and-pontifical-university-of-mexico.jpg" alt="Royal and Pontifical University of Mexico" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VegaMex (Óscar Vega) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Escudo_de_la_Real_y_Pontificia_Universidad_de_M%C3%A9xico.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
